From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:41:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Drop the unused interval specialization of the epoch statistics

DatePartUnaryStatistics() wires PropagateStatistics up for the
timestamp, date and time types only, so the explicit specialization
of DatePart::EpochOperator::PropagateStatistics for interval_t, which
returned no statistics, is never referenced, and inside the file's
anonymous namespace -Wunused-function reports it as defined but not
used. It goes, with the comment that explained why it returned
nullptr: the interval epoch has no statistics propagation to opt out
of.

Carried here until upstream takes it; not proposed upstream yet.

---
 src/duckdb/extension/core_functions/scalar/date/date_part.cpp | 7 -------
 1 file changed, 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/duckdb/extension/core_functions/scalar/date/date_part.cpp b/src/duckdb/extension/core_functions/scalar/date/date_part.cpp
index 7c4b4823aa..dbf07fd03f 100644
--- a/src/duckdb/extension/core_functions/scalar/date/date_part.cpp
+++ b/src/duckdb/extension/core_functions/scalar/date/date_part.cpp
@@ -1484,13 +1484,6 @@ double DatePart::EpochOperator::Operation(interval_t input) {
 	return double(interval_epoch) + double(input.micros) / double(Interval::MICROS_PER_SEC);
 }
 
-//	TODO: We can't propagate interval statistics because we can't easily compare interval_t for order.
-template <>
-unique_ptr<BaseStatistics> DatePart::EpochOperator::PropagateStatistics<interval_t>(ClientContext &context,
-                                                                                    FunctionStatisticsInput &input) {
-	return nullptr;
-}
-
 template <>
 double DatePart::EpochOperator::Operation(dtime_t input) {
 	return double(input.value) / double(Interval::MICROS_PER_SEC);
